# Critique expansion (patches): lung, GI, GU.
PATCH = {}
def c(name, critique, exc=None):
    PATCH[name] = {"critique": critique}
    if exc: PATCH[name]["exclusion"] = exc

c("RTOG 0617", "60 Gy remains the standard dose for concurrent chemoRT. The unexpected harm from 74 Gy is attributed partly to higher heart and lung doses and treatment interruptions, which made cardiac dose constraints a core part of thoracic planning. Cetuximab added toxicity without benefit.")
c("PARAMOUNT", "Established continuation pemetrexed maintenance, which survives today as part of chemo-pembrolizumab regimens. Because only patients without progression after induction were randomised, the absolute benefit applies to a selected group.")
c("Scagliotti cis-pem vs cis-gem", "Made histology essential for chemotherapy choice — pemetrexed for non-squamous, gemcitabine for squamous. The finding came from a prespecified subgroup analysis in a non-inferiority trial but was biologically plausible (thymidylate synthase expression) and has been reproduced.")
c("LIBRETTO-431", "Supports first-line selpercatinib and broad NGS panels at diagnosis. Using a chemo-pembrolizumab control makes this a strong design, but RET fusions are rare (~1–2%) and OS is immature, so long-term benefit and sequencing data are still evolving.")
c("DESTINY-Lung01/02", "Led to accelerated approval of T-DXd for HER2-mutant NSCLC. The 5.4 mg/kg dose kept efficacy while lowering ILD; without randomised data, its superiority over docetaxel-based therapy is inferred rather than proven.")
c("CONVERT", "Twice-daily 45 Gy remains the reference, but 66 Gy once daily gave similar survival and toxicity, so it is acceptable when twice-daily treatment is impractical — a common issue in Indian centres with heavy machine loads. CALGB 30610 reached a similar conclusion for 70 Gy.")
c("Turrisi INT 0096", "Basis for 45 Gy twice daily in LS-SCLC. The comparator of 45 Gy once daily was biologically low, so the trial proves accelerated fractionation beats an under-dosed schedule rather than a modern once-daily dose.")
c("ADRIATIC", "Durvalumab consolidation is a new standard in LS-SCLC with a clear OS gain. As in PACIFIC, randomisation after chemoRT selects patients who responded and remained fit, and pneumonitis risk after thoracic RT plus IO needs vigilance.")
c("IMpower133", "Chemo-atezolizumab is first-line standard for ES-SCLC. The median OS gain is only ~2 months, but a tail of long-term survivors justifies its use; biomarkers to select those patients are lacking.")
c("CASPIAN", "Confirms chemo-IO as first-line standard in ES-SCLC and allowed cisplatin, which is useful where carboplatin access or renal function is an issue. The tremelimumab arm added toxicity without benefit.")
c("DeLLphi-304", "Tarlatamab is a new second-line standard in SCLC with an OS gain over chemotherapy. CRS and ICANS require monitoring during the first cycles, which limits use in centres without inpatient support.")
c("MAGIC", "Established perioperative chemotherapy for gastro-oesophageal adenocarcinoma. Poor completion of postoperative chemotherapy and limited surgical lymphadenectomy mean the benefit may partly reflect preoperative therapy alone; FLOT has since replaced ECF.")
c("KEYNOTE-811", "Adds pembrolizumab to trastuzumab-chemotherapy for HER2+ gastric cancer. The benefit is confined to PD-L1 CPS ≥1, so PD-L1 testing is needed; early approval on response rates was later supported by survival data.")
c("CheckMate 649", "Nivolumab plus chemotherapy is the standard for HER2-negative gastric/GEJ adenocarcinoma, but the benefit is concentrated in CPS ≥5. In CPS <1 tumours the gain is negligible, and regulators in Europe restricted use accordingly.")
c("SPOTLIGHT / GLOW", "Zolbetuximab adds a targeted option for CLDN18.2-positive disease (~35–40% of gastric cancers). Nausea and vomiting are common, and as neither trial included immunotherapy, the best sequence with nivolumab is unknown.")
c("RAINBOW", "Ramucirumab-paclitaxel remains a second-line standard. The OS gain is modest, and in India, weekly paclitaxel alone is often used because of cost.")
c("CheckMate 648 / KEYNOTE-590", "Chemo-IO is first-line standard for oesophageal SCC, the dominant histology in India. Benefit is driven by PD-L1-positive tumours, so testing matters; nivolumab-ipilimumab without chemotherapy is an alternative in CheckMate 648.")
c("RTOG 85-01", "Basis for definitive chemoRT in oesophageal cancer, showing long-term survival was possible without surgery. The RT-alone arm (64 Gy) had no 5-year survivors, making the case for concurrent chemotherapy.")
c("MOSAIC", "FOLFOX is standard adjuvant therapy for stage III colon cancer. The OS benefit in stage II was not significant, so oxaliplatin is reserved for high-risk stage II; persistent neuropathy is an important long-term cost.")
c("IDEA", "3 months of CAPOX is standard for low-risk stage III (T1–3 N1), while high-risk disease may still need 6 months. The regimen effect (CAPOX better than FOLFOX for short duration) was not randomised, so it is debated.")
c("ATOMIC", "Adding atezolizumab to FOLFOX for stage III dMMR colon cancer greatly reduces recurrence. With neoadjuvant IO results (NICHE-2) so striking, the future may be IO before surgery rather than adjuvant combination therapy.")
c("NICHE-2", "Strong signal for neoadjuvant IO in dMMR colon cancer with very high pathological response after only two doses. As a single-arm study, it needs randomised confirmation and long-term outcomes.")
c("Cercek dostarlimab dMMR rectal", "Paradigm-shifting: all early patients achieved clinical complete response without surgery or RT. The cohort is small and highly selected, so durable organ preservation needs longer follow-up and rigorous surveillance.")
c("German CAO/ARO/AIO-94", "Preoperative chemoRT became standard because it halved local recurrence and caused less toxicity than postoperative chemoRT. OS was not improved, and the risk of overtreating MRI-overstaged tumours remains.")
c("Dutch TME", "Short-course RT reduces local recurrence even with quality-assured TME surgery. The benefit is largely in mid/low rectal tumours, and long-term bowel and sexual dysfunction must be weighed.")
c("RAPIDO", "Supports total neoadjuvant therapy with short-course RT for high-risk rectal cancer, doubling pCR. Higher locoregional recurrence in long-term follow-up, especially after breached mesorectum, has tempered enthusiasm.")
c("PRODIGE 23", "Intensified TNT with FOLFIRINOX improved DFS and later OS. It is best suited to young, fit patients; toxicity and resource needs limit broader use.")
c("CRYSTAL", "Established anti-EGFR therapy for RAS wild-type mCRC. Its biomarker findings were retrospective, but they set the foundation for RAS testing before cetuximab or panitumumab.")
c("FIRE-3 / CALGB 80405", "Anti-EGFR therapy is preferred for left-sided RAS wild-type tumours and bevacizumab for right-sided. The sidedness effect was found retrospectively, but PARADIGM later confirmed it prospectively.")
c("PARADIGM", "Panitumumab-FOLFOX is standard first-line for left-sided RAS/BRAF wild-type mCRC. Data come from a Japanese population, but the biology of sidedness appears consistent worldwide.")
c("CheckMate 8HW", "Nivolumab-ipilimumab improved PFS over both chemotherapy and nivolumab alone in MSI-H mCRC. Greater immune toxicity and immature OS mean pembrolizumab monotherapy remains a reasonable choice.")
c("BEACON CRC / BREAKWATER", "Encorafenib-cetuximab improves OS in BRAF V600E mCRC, a poor-prognosis group. Adding binimetinib gave no further benefit, and BREAKWATER moves the doublet with FOLFOX to first line.")
c("DESTINY-CRC / MOUNTAINEER", "Supports HER2 testing in RAS wild-type mCRC (~3–5% HER2-positive). Single-arm data give high response rates, but randomised evidence and cost-effectiveness are lacking.")
c("CORRECT / RECOURSE / SUNLIGHT", "FTD-TPI with bevacizumab is preferred in later lines because SUNLIGHT showed meaningful OS gain over FTD-TPI alone. Regorafenib's toxicity (hand-foot syndrome, fatigue) often requires dose-escalation strategies (ReDOS).")
c("FRESCO-2", "Fruquintinib adds a late-line option with modest OS benefit. It is generally better tolerated than regorafenib, but cost is a barrier.")
c("EORTC 40983 (EPOC)", "Perioperative FOLFOX improves PFS for resectable liver metastases but not OS. It remains a reasonable option; adding cetuximab (New EPOC) was harmful.")
c("RTOG 98-11", "Confirmed 5-FU/mitomycin chemoRT as standard for anal cancer; cisplatin induction delayed RT and worsened colostomy-free survival.")
c("POD1UM-303", "First positive immunotherapy trial in advanced anal SCC, adding retifanlimab to carboplatin-paclitaxel. OS data are awaited, and access to the drug is limited outside trials.")
c("REFLECT", "Lenvatinib is non-inferior to sorafenib with better response and PFS. It remains a first-line option when immunotherapy is unsuitable, though high-risk patients were excluded.")
c("HIMALAYA", "STRIDE gives durable OS benefit with a single tremelimumab priming dose. It avoids bevacizumab, making it useful for patients with bleeding risk or varices.")
c("CheckMate 9DW", "Nivolumab-ipilimumab adds a first-line option for HCC. More early deaths in the IO arm suggest careful selection of fit patients.")
c("TOPAZ-1", "Durvalumab with gem-cis is first-line standard for biliary tract cancer. The median OS gain is modest, but a tail of long-term survivors exists; gallbladder cancer, common in North India, showed less benefit.")
c("ABC-02", "Established gem-cis as the standard chemotherapy backbone for advanced biliary cancer, on which immunotherapy has since been added.")
c("BILCAP", "Capecitabine is standard adjuvant therapy for resected biliary cancer. The ITT analysis was negative, so the evidence relies on per-protocol and sensitivity analyses.")
c("ClarIDHy / FIGHT-202", "Supports NGS in intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, where IDH1 and FGFR2 alterations are targetable. Benefits are modest and drug access in India is limited.")
c("NETTER-1", "PRRT is the standard second-line therapy for progressive midgut NET. The PFS benefit is large, but the final OS difference was not statistically significant, partly due to crossover.")
c("NETTER-2", "First-line PRRT improves PFS in higher-grade (G2/G3) GEP-NETs. OS data are immature and access to Lu-177 DOTATATE is limited in India.")
c("PROMID / CLARINET", "SSAs are first-line antiproliferative therapy for well-differentiated NETs, with modest PFS benefit and good tolerability.")
c("RADIANT-3/4 & SUN-1111", "Everolimus and sunitinib are options after SSAs, chosen by site and toxicity profile. Neither improved OS significantly.")
c("E2211 (CAPTEM)", "CAPTEM improves PFS and OS over temozolomide alone in pancreatic NETs. It is an inexpensive oral regimen well suited to Indian practice.")
c("Bolla EORTC 22863", "Established long-term ADT with RT for high-risk prostate cancer. The RT dose (70 Gy) is lower than modern standards, but the benefit of 2–3 years ADT has been confirmed in later trials.")
c("CHHiP", "60 Gy in 20 fractions is now standard moderate hypofractionation, saving three weeks of treatment. Most patients also received short-course ADT, which may have influenced outcomes.")
c("HYPO-RT-PC / PACE-B", "Five-fraction SBRT is a standard option for low- and intermediate-risk prostate cancer, with non-inferior biochemical control. Higher acute urinary toxicity in HYPO-RT-PC was transient.")
c("ASCENDE-RT", "LDR brachytherapy boost nearly halved biochemical failure. Higher late urinary toxicity and lack of OS benefit mean careful patient selection.")
c("RADICALS-RT / RAVES / GETUG-AFU 17 (ARTISTIC)", "Early salvage RT is preferred over routine adjuvant RT after prostatectomy, avoiding RT in many men. Few very high-risk patients were included, so adjuvant RT may still benefit them.")
c("STAMPEDE (arm H) M1 RT", "Prostate RT improves OS in low-volume mHSPC. It is not effective in high-volume disease; volume is defined by conventional imaging, not PSMA-PET.")
c("CHAARTED", "Docetaxel with ADT improves OS in high-volume mHSPC, but not clearly in low-volume disease. Triplet therapy has since evolved from this foundation.")
c("LATITUDE / STAMPEDE abiraterone", "Abiraterone with ADT improves OS in high-risk mHSPC. Inexpensive generics make it highly relevant in India, but hypertension and hypokalaemia need monitoring.")
c("TITAN / ENZAMET / ARCHES", "ARPIs with ADT improve OS in mHSPC across disease volumes. Fatigue, falls and cost are the main considerations.")
c("PEACE-1 / ARASENS", "Triplet therapy (ADT + docetaxel + ARPI) improves OS over ADT + docetaxel in de novo mHSPC. No trial compares triplet with ADT + ARPI doublet, which is the key unanswered question.")
c("SPARTAN / PROSPER / ARAMIS", "ARPIs delay metastasis and improve OS in high-risk nmCRPC. With PSMA-PET, most such patients would now be reclassified as metastatic.")
c("EMBARK", "Enzalutamide with ADT improves metastasis-free survival in high-risk biochemical recurrence. OS data and PSMA-PET staging will shape its use.")
c("TAX 327", "Docetaxel was the first chemotherapy to prolong survival in mCRPC. The 3-weekly schedule became standard.")
c("COU-AA-301/302, AFFIRM, PREVAIL", "Abiraterone and enzalutamide improve OS in mCRPC before and after docetaxel. Cross-resistance means switching ARPIs is less effective.")
c("TROPIC / CARD", "Cabazitaxel improves OS after docetaxel and is better than switching ARPI (CARD). Neutropenia requires G-CSF prophylaxis.")
c("ALSYMPCA", "Radium-223 improves OS in bone-only mCRPC. Combination with abiraterone increased fractures (ERA 223), so bone health agents are recommended.")
c("VISION", "Lu-PSMA improves OS in heavily pretreated mCRPC. The control arm excluded chemotherapy and had early dropouts, which may favour Lu-PSMA.")
c("PSMAfore", "Lu-PSMA before docetaxel improves rPFS, but OS is confounded by crossover. It is an option for patients unfit or unwilling for chemotherapy.")
c("PROfound", "Olaparib improves OS in mCRPC with BRCA1/2 or ATM alterations. Benefit in other HRR genes is uncertain, and the control arm was ARPI switch.")
c("TALAPRO-2 / PROpel", "PARPi + ARPI improves rPFS in first-line mCRPC, mainly in HRR-mutated patients. Use in HRR-negative patients is debated.")
c("SWOG 8710", "Established neoadjuvant cisplatin-based chemotherapy for MIBC, improving survival. Toxicity and slow accrual reflect the challenges of neoadjuvant therapy.")
c("VESPER", "Dose-dense MVAC gives better PFS than gem-cis. Toxicity is higher, so patient selection matters.")
c("NIAGARA", "Perioperative durvalumab with gem-cis improves EFS and OS in MIBC. The contribution of adjuvant therapy is unclear.")
c("CheckMate 274", "Adjuvant nivolumab improves DFS in high-risk MIUC. OS data are awaited.")
c("BC2001", "Chemoradiotherapy with 5-FU/MMC improves locoregional control for bladder preservation. OS was not improved, but bladder preservation is a key quality-of-life benefit.")
c("POUT", "Adjuvant chemotherapy improves DFS in UTUC. Carboplatin was used for patients with poor renal function.")
c("JAVELIN Bladder 100", "Avelumab maintenance improves OS after platinum. Its role has diminished with EV-pembro first-line.")
c("EV-302", "EV-pembro doubles OS over platinum chemotherapy in first-line advanced UC. Rash, neuropathy and hyperglycaemia require monitoring.")
c("CheckMate 901 (gem-cis + nivo)", "Nivolumab with gem-cis improves OS in cisplatin-eligible advanced UC. EV-pembro is now preferred.")
c("THOR", "Erdafitinib improves OS in FGFR-altered UC after ARPI/IO. Hyperphosphataemia and ocular toxicity need monitoring.")
c("CheckMate 214", "Nivo-ipi gives durable OS benefit in intermediate/poor risk ccRCC. No benefit in favourable risk.")
c("KEYNOTE-426 / CLEAR / CheckMate 9ER", "IO-TKI combinations improve OS over sunitinib in advanced ccRCC. Toxicity from TKIs requires dose management.")
c("KEYNOTE-564", "Adjuvant pembrolizumab improves DFS and OS in high-risk ccRCC. Some patients are overtreated.")
c("CARMENA", "Cytoreductive nephrectomy is not mandatory in intermediate/poor risk mRCC. Poor accrual limits conclusions.")
c("LITESPARK-005", "Belzutifan improves PFS in advanced ccRCC after IO and VEGF-TKI. OS was not improved.")
c("Einhorn PVB / BEP (Williams)", "BEP replaced PVB with less neuromuscular toxicity and equivalent efficacy. It remains the standard for germ cell tumours.")
c("IGCCCG", "Defined prognostic groups that guide risk-adapted therapy. It remains the basis for treatment decisions.")
c("MRC TE19 (Oliver)", "Single-dose carboplatin is non-inferior to RT for stage I seminoma. Surveillance is now preferred for low-risk patients.")
